        rules: {
          objective: 'Win tricks containing point cards. Reach a target score (typically 61 or more of 120 total points) to win the hand.',
          gameplay: "1. **Dealing**: Cards dealt to all players.\n2. **Bidding/Contract**: Players may bid for the right to name trumps or play a special contract.\n3. **Trump Selection**: The declarer chooses the trump suit (or plays a no-trump/solo contract).\n4. **Trick Play**: Player to dealer's left leads. Must follow suit if possible. If void, may play any card (trump or discard).\n5. **Winning Tricks**: Highest trump wins, or highest card of led suit. Winner leads to next trick.",
          keyRules: [
            'Must follow suit if possible; if void, may trump or discard.',
            'Obers and Unters are permanent trumps above the named trump suit.',
            'Declarer side needs 61 or more of 120 card points to win the hand.',
            'In Rufer, declarer calls a non-trump Ace; its holder is the silent partner.',
            'Forehand (left of dealer) leads to the first trick; trick winner leads next.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Count your high cards (Aces, Tens, Kings). These are your point winners. Try to capture opponents' high cards while protecting your own.",
          intermediate: "Trump management is key. With many trumps, lead them to draw out opponents' trumps. With few trumps, save them for crucial tricks. Watch which trumps have been played.",
          advanced: "Signal to your partner through your card play. Leading certain cards can indicate strength or weakness in suits. In partnership games, cooperation is essential - don't undermine your partner's winning cards.",
          tips: [
            'Aces and Tens are the primary point cards - prioritize capturing these.',
            'The Ober/Unter (Queen/Jack) trumps are powerful - use them wisely.',
            'Communication with your partner through play is crucial in partnership games.',
            'Consider the score situation when deciding whether to bid or play safe.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
